arXiv:2602.21895v1 [math.CO] 25 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> and related conjectures
</span></h1>

<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">Julien Cassaigne 
<br class="ltx_break">CNRS, I2M UMR 7373, Aix-Marseille Université, 13453 Marseille, France 
<br class="ltx_break"><a href="mailto:%20Julien.Cassaigne@math.cnrs.fr" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">Julien.Cassaigne@math.cnrs.fr</a></span></p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">and</span></p>
<p id="p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">Bastián Espinoza, Michel Rigo, Manon Stipulanti
<br class="ltx_break">Department of Mathematics, University of Liège
<br class="ltx_break">Allée de la Découverte 12 (B37), 4000 Liège, Belgium
<br class="ltx_break"><a href="mailto:%20BAEspinoza@uliege.be" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">BAEspinoza@uliege.be</a>, <a href="mailto:%20M.Rigo@uliege.be" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">M.Rigo@uliege.be</a>, <a href="mailto:%20M.Stipulanti@uliege.be" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">M.Stipulanti@uliege.be</a>
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> rational base numeration systems; block substitutions; Thue–Morse sequence; frequency; uniform recurrence; Pontryagin duality; Fourier coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification:</span> 68R15, 43A65, 11B85, 11A63</p>
</div>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study a binary Thue–Morse-type sequence arising from the base-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> expansion of integers, an archetypal automatic sequence in a rational base numeration system. Because the sequence is generated by a periodic iteration of morphisms rather than a single primitive substitution, classical Perron–Frobenius methods do not directly apply to determine symbol frequencies. We prove that both symbols <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0},{\tt 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0},{\tt 1}</annotation></semantics></math> occur with frequency <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> and we show uniform recurrence and symmetry properties of its set of factors. The proof reveals a structural bridge between combinatorics on words and harmonic analysis: the first difference sequence is shown to be Toeplitz, providing dynamical rigidity, while filtered frequencies naturally encode a dyadic structure that lifts to the compact group of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic integers. In this <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic setting, desubstitution becomes a linear operator on Fourier coefficients, and a spectral contraction argument enforces uniqueness of limiting densities. Our results answer several conjectures of Dekking (on a sibling sequence) and illustrate how harmonic analysis on compact groups can be fruitfully combined with substitution dynamics.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a sequence <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}=(s_{n})_{n\geq 0}\in A^{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>ℕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}=(s_{n})_{n\geq 0}\in A^{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> over a finite alphabet <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, two fundamental questions concern the existence and the computation of the frequency of occurrences of a symbol <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely the quantity</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{\#\{0\leq i&lt;N\mid s_{i}=a\}}{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{\#\{0\leq i&lt;N\mid s_{i}=a\}}{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Answering these questions has applications in several fields, e.g., invariant measures in symbolic dynamics and ergodic theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, normality in number theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, structure and balance properties in combinatorics on words <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, data compression and pattern prediction in information theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, densities in aperiodic tilings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, our initial motivation was to answer a conjecture that has been open for five years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. It concerns the symbol frequencies in the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thue–Morse word in base <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}={\tt 001110111110110111110000110110}\cdots," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟶𝟶𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}={\tt 001110111110110111110000110110}\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">an infinite sequence generated by a particular type of substitution obtained by periodically iterating two morphisms of constant length <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>; see <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Periodically iterated morphisms ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> for precise definitions. Focusing on this sequence is natural: it is a typical automatic sequence in a rational base (here <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>). Although generated by simple rules, it exhibits a rich and non-trivial structure that reflects the complexity of the object. We prove that the frequency of <span id="S1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0</span> is indeed <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> — as suggested by numerical computations as pictured in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> — but we obtain more: we prove that <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly recurrent and its set of factors is closed under bit-wise complement and reversal. In contrast with classical primitive substitutive systems, <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> arises from a periodically iterated morphism and mixes two scaling mechanisms, preventing a direct application of Perron–Frobenius theory and requiring a different approach to establish the existence of symbol frequencies. Compared with integer base systems or substitutive systems, the language of the base-<math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> numeration system is highly non-trivial: any two distinct infinite subtrees are non-isomorphic, which is an important difference to overcome.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21895v1/freq1.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:308/197;" width="308" height="197"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21895v1/freq2.svg" id="S1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:312/197;" width="312" height="197"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Estimation of the frequency of <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">0</span> in prefixes of the Thue–Morse word <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in base <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>: on the left, for prefixes with length <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 2000</annotation></semantics></math>; on the right, those with length in <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[14000,15000]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>14000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15000</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[14000,15000]</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also answer several conjectures related to a sibling sequence <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced by Dekking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and recalled in <a href="#S1.SS4" title="1.4 Dekking’s variation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. As already mentioned before, these sequences <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are linked to the theory of numeration systems. The symbols of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed from the expansions of integers in a rational base numeration system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. More precisely, for any integer <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th symbol of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the parity of the sum-of-digits of <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in its base-<math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> expansion. Rational base numeration systems, introduced by Akiyama, Frougny, and Sakarovitch in 2008 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and initially related to a question of Mahler about the distribution of the fractional parts of <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\,(3/2)^{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\,(3/2)^{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is a real, have attracted the attention of many researchers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. Our solution using techniques from abstract harmonic analysis suggests possible extensions to similar problems and, in particular, sheds new light on long-standing conjectures related to the Oldenburger–Kolakoski word <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>; see <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Periodically iterated morphisms ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> for its definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The study of substitutions and morphic words lies at the crossroads of combinatorics on words and symbolic dynamics, but also harmonic analysis. Beyond their combinatorial structure, substitutions generate dynamical systems whose spectral properties encode arithmetic and structural information. Tools from abstract harmonic analysis, such as Fourier–Stieltjes transforms, spectral measures, and Riesz products, play a central role in the analysis of these systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. In particular, the spectral theory of substitutive dynamical systems provides a framework to distinguish pure point, singular continuous, and absolutely continuous behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let us give a few more examples highlighting the interactions between these seemingly distant topics. Baake and Grimm derive a Fourier recursion and functional equation for the Thue–Morse autocorrelation measure, yielding an explicit Riesz product representation and a purely singular continuous spectrum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Still related to the Thue–Morse word, uniform exponential bounds are obtained for discrete Fourier coefficients of truncated Thue–Morse sums, averaged over arithmetic parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. Finally, automatic sequences admit an efficient decomposition into a structured component and a Gowers-uniform component, showing via higher-order Fourier analysis that sequences orthogonal to periodic ones have small Gowers norms and thus behave pseudorandomly with respect to additive patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is organized as follows. In <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Substitutions or iterated morphisms ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> we briefly recall the notion of substitutions and fixed points. Then, in <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Periodically iterated morphisms ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, the generation of infinite words by substitutions is extended to the case where a finite number of morphisms are applied periodically. We recall that the famous Oldenburger–Kolakoski sequence can be obtained in this way and list the main conjectures about it. In <a href="#S1.SS3" title="1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we briefly introduce the base-<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> numeration system and the corresponding Thue–Morse sequence <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by the variation proposed by Dekking in <a href="#S1.SS4" title="1.4 Dekking’s variation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. Finally, in <a href="#S1.SS5" title="1.5 Organization of the paper and our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, we talk about the organization of the rest of the paper and we list the main contributions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Substitutions or iterated morphisms</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an alphabet <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of finite words over <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Endowed with concatenation product, it is a monoid with the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empty word <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></em> as neutral element.
To distinguish finite and infinite words, the latter are written in bold.
A map <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:A^{*}\to A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:A^{*}\to A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">morphism</span> if it is a homomorphism of monoids, i.e., <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(uv)=f(u)f(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(uv)=f(u)f(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an infinite word <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and integers <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq j</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}[i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>) denote the factor <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{i}x_{i+1}\cdots x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}x_{i+1}\cdots x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{i}x_{i+1}\cdots x_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}x_{i+1}\cdots x_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}[0,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}[0,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is the length-<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> prefix of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a word and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a symbol in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u|_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u|_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of occurrences of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. This notation extends to factors: if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite word, then <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u|_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u|_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of occurrences of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> as a factor of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by recalling the notion of iterated morphisms.
It is a classical method for generating infinite words. We specify the main definitions and start with the (standard) <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thue–Morse sequence</span> <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}=(t_{n})_{n\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}=(t_{n})_{n\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="https://oeis.org/A010060" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_underline">A010060</a>) starting with</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{t}={\tt 0110100110010110}\cdots." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}={\tt 0110100110010110}\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This element of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined by <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}=\mathbf{s}_{2}(n)\bmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}=\mathbf{s}_{2}(n)\bmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum-of-digits function in base <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. The Thue–Morse sequence satisfies the relations</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2n}=t_{n}\quad\text{ and }\quad t_{2n+1}=1-t_{n},\quad\forall n\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext> and </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2n}=t_{n}\quad\text{ and }\quad t_{2n+1}=1-t_{n},\quad\forall n\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Since every length-<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> factor <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2n}t_{2n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2n}t_{2n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, occurring in an even position, is either <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟶𝟷</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 01}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟷𝟶</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 10}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is trivial to see that the frequency of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟶</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟷</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 1}</annotation></semantics></math>) is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. This sequence is an example of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-automatic sequences: for all <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of a deterministic finite automaton fed with the base-<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> expansion of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. It is thus the fixed point of a <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-uniform morphism, namely <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\tt 0}\mapsto{\tt 01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\tt 0}\mapsto{\tt 01}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟷𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 10}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by iterating <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟶</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sequence of finite words <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{n}({\tt 0}))_{n\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{n}({\tt 0}))_{n\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, for the product topology, where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> has the discrete topology:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 01}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 01},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{2}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{2}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 0110}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 0110},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{3}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{3}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 01101001}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷𝟷𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟶𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 01101001},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Indeed, the length <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f^{n}({\tt 0})|=2^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f^{n}({\tt 0})|=2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity with <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and each image <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{n}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{n}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a prefix of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{n+1}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{n+1}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a survey on the Thue–Morse word, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. One of its well-known combinatorial features is that it is overlap-free: it contains no factor of the form <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="auaua" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">auaua</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a symbol and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite word. For an integer <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, a sequence is <em id="S1.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS1.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-automatic</em> if it is the image under a <em id="S1.SS1.p2.4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coding</em> (i.e., a letter-to-letter morphism) of a fixed point of a <em id="S1.SS1.p2.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS1.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-uniform</em> morphism (i.e., the length of the image of every letter is <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>).
For references on automatic sequences and combinatorics on words, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Cobham showed in 1972 that for an automatic sequence, if the frequency of a symbol exists, then it is a rational number <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
The larger class of morphic sequences is obtained by relaxing the assumption that the morphism generating the word has constant length. In that situation, if the frequency of a symbol exists, then it is an algebraic number <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Thm. 8.4.5]</cite>. Using Perron–Frobenius theory for primitive matrices, frequencies of symbols exist and can be obtained as the normalized Perron eigenvector of the adjacency matrix associated with the morphism, i.e., where the <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column records the number of occurrences of each symbol in the image of the letter <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, the Tribonacci word <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\tt 01020100102}\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟸𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟶𝟷𝟶𝟸</mn><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 01020100102}\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="https://oeis.org/A080843" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_underline">A080843</a>) is the fixed point of the morphism <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0}\mapsto{\tt 01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟶𝟷</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0}\mapsto{\tt 01}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 02}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟶𝟸</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 02}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and its adjacency matrix is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;1\\
1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;1\\
1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\\
\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Periodically iterated morphisms</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One may generalize the construction of morphic sequences by replacing a single morphism with a finite family of several morphisms applied periodically. This construction produces a larger class of infinite words <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer, let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite alphabet, and let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> morphisms over <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. An infinite word <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an <span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternating fixed point</span> of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1})</annotation></semantics></math> if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}=f_{0}(x_{0})f_{1}(x_{1})\cdots f_{r-1}(x_{r-1})f_{0}(x_{r})\cdots f_{i\bmod{r}}(x_{i})\cdots." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>mod</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=f_{0}(x_{0})f_{1}(x_{1})\cdots f_{r-1}(x_{r-1})f_{0}(x_{r})\cdots f_{i\bmod{r}}(x_{i})\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the literature, one also finds the terminology of <em id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">periodic iteration of morphisms</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The famous <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Oldenburger–Kolakoski word</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{k}={\tt 2211212212211}\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟷𝟸𝟷𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟷</mn><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}={\tt 2211212212211}\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> (shift of <a href="https://oeis.org/A000002" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_underline">A000002</a> where the first <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">1</span> has been conveniently deleted) can be obtained by periodically iterating the two morphisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 2},\\
{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 2}{\tt 2},\\
\end{array}\right.\quad\text{ and }\quad k_{1}:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 1},\\
{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 1}{\tt 1}.\\
\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟸</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext> and </mtext></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>𝟷𝟷</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 2},\\
{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 2}{\tt 2},\\
\end{array}\right.\quad\text{ and }\quad k_{1}:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 1}\mapsto{\tt 1},\\
{\tt 2}\mapsto{\tt 1}{\tt 1}.\\
\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The first few iterations give</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 22}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 22},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})k_{1}({\tt 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})k_{1}({\tt 2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 2211}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 2211},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})k_{1}({\tt 2})k_{0}({\tt 1})k_{1}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{0}({\tt 2})k_{1}({\tt 2})k_{0}({\tt 1})k_{1}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\tt 221121}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟷𝟸𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\tt 221121},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">It is the unique word <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> starting with <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{RL}(\mathbf{k})=\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐤</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{RL}(\mathbf{k})=\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{RL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖱𝖫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{RL}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S1.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">run-length encoding map</em>. It is a challenging object of study in combinatorics on words. To account for this, we recall several long-standing conjectures concerning <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is conjectured that both letters occur with frequency <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. The best bounds from Rao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> improving on Chvátal are</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.49992\leq\liminf_{N}\frac{|\mathbf{k}[0,N)|_{2}}{N}\leq\limsup_{N}\frac{|\mathbf{k}[0,N)|_{2}}{N}\leq 0.50008." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.49992</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim inf</mo><mi>N</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim sup</mo><mi>N</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.50008</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.49992\leq\liminf_{N}\frac{|\mathbf{k}[0,N)|_{2}}{N}\leq\limsup_{N}\frac{|\mathbf{k}[0,N)|_{2}}{N}\leq 0.50008.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is unknown whether every factor occurs infinitely often in <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recurrence</em>), or even with bounded gaps (i.e., <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform recurrence</em>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reversal</span> of a word <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1}\cdots a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\cdots a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the word <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{n}\cdots a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\cdots a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in A</annotation></semantics></math>. It is open whether the set of factors of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under reversal or complement (exchanging <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟷</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟸</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the factor complexity of <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to be bounded by a polynomial, its precise growth remains unclear (recall that, for an infinite sequence <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, its <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">factor complexity</em> <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives, for each integer <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of length-<math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> factors of <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Oldenburger–Kolakoski sequences over larger alphabets or with different parameters, analogous questions concerning frequencies, recurrence, and structural properties are largely open. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> for a recent probabilistic perspective and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> where pseudo-substitutions are iterated not necessarily in a periodic way as in <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 1. ‣ 1.2 Periodically iterated morphisms ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As observed by Dekking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> for the Oldenburger–Kolakoski word <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, an alternating fixed point can also be obtained by an <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-block substitution as defined below. For any integer <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of length-<math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> words over <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer and let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite alphabet. An <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-block substitution</span> <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:A^{r}\to A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:A^{r}\to A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> maps a word <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{0}\cdots w_{rn-1}\in A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}\cdots w_{rn-1}\in A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta(w_{0}\cdots w_{r-1})\beta(w_{r}\cdots w_{2r-1})\cdots\beta(w_{r(n-1)}\cdots w_{rn-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(w_{0}\cdots w_{r-1})\beta(w_{r}\cdots w_{2r-1})\cdots\beta(w_{r(n-1)}\cdots w_{rn-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If the length of the word is not a multiple of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, then the remaining suffix is ignored under the action of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. An infinite word <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{n})_{n\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fixed point of the <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-block substitution</span> <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta:A^{r}\to A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta:A^{r}\to A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> if it satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}=\beta(x_{0}\cdots x_{r-1})\beta(x_{r}\cdots x_{2r-1})\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=\beta(x_{0}\cdots x_{r-1})\beta(x_{r}\cdots x_{2r-1})\cdots</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer, let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite alphabet, and let <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> morphisms over <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to see that if an infinite word over <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an alternating fixed point of <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{0},\ldots,f_{r-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is a fixed point of an <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-block substitution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. As an example, <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐤</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed point of the <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-block substitution given by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 11}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 1})h_{1}({\tt 1})={\tt 21},\\
{\tt 12}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 1})h_{1}({\tt 2})={\tt 211},\\
{\tt 21}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 2})h_{1}({\tt 1})={\tt 221},\\
{\tt 22}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 2})h_{1}({\tt 2})={\tt 2211}.\\
\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟷𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟷𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟷𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟸𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸𝟷</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟸𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟸</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟸𝟸𝟷𝟷</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}{\tt 11}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 1})h_{1}({\tt 1})={\tt 21},\\
{\tt 12}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 1})h_{1}({\tt 2})={\tt 211},\\
{\tt 21}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 2})h_{1}({\tt 1})={\tt 221},\\
{\tt 22}\mapsto h_{0}({\tt 2})h_{1}({\tt 2})={\tt 2211}.\\
\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>The base <math id="S1.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding Thue–Morse word</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall that, in the base-<math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math> numeration system, any positive integer <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is written</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=\sum_{i\geq 0}d_{i}\,\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=\sum_{i\geq 0}d_{i}\,\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with digits <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. The <em id="S1.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>-expansion</em> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle n\rangle_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle n\rangle_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By convention, the empty word <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>-expansion of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <a href="#S1.T1" title="In 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the first few expansions.</p>
</div>
