<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>For each integer <math id="S1.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in[0,26]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in[0,26]</annotation></semantics></math>, are displayed the <math id="S1.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>-expansion <math id="S1.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle n\rangle_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle n\rangle_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of the sum-of-digits <math id="S1.T1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> in base <math id="S1.T1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding value of <math id="S1.T1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> modulo <math id="S1.T1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the symbol <math id="S1.T1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Thue–Morse word <math id="S1.T1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in base <math id="S1.T1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A convenient way to visualize <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/2</annotation></semantics></math>-expansions in this numeration system is to construct the associated tree (see <a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
In this tree, under a breadth-first traversal, the vertices have degree <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> alternately. For vertices of degree <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, the outgoing edges are labeled <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, while for vertices of degree 1, the unique outgoing edge is labeled <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the tree is constructed by an elementary and periodic process — a <em id="S1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">periodic rhythm</em>. For each integer <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the path from the root to the <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th visited vertex in breadth-first search represents <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in the numeration system: its label is <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle n\rangle_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle n\rangle_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. To avoid expansions starting with <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is assumed that the root has a (hidden) loop of label <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that any two adjacent vertices are the parents of three vertices. This phenomenon explains why <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-block substitutions with images of length <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> naturally arise in this context.</p>
</div>
