)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi^{3}(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,j))|=n_{3}(j)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Using the fixed point structure, we iterate the map <math id="S2.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> three times on successive prefixes of <math id="S2.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now track how the parity of positions evolves under iterations of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, let</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n_{k}(j):=|\Phi^{k}(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,j))|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j):=|\Phi^{k}(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,j))|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}(j)=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}(j)=j</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
These quantities allow us to determine which of the two morphisms <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied at a given position.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">To give some intuition, let us track the successive images of the letter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when applying <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, symbolized by the gray rectangles in <a href="#S2.F5" title="In Proof. ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is even, we apply <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the image of <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(t_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(t_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. The position where this image occurs is given by <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}(2)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}(2)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(t_{2})=t_{3}t_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(t_{2})=t_{3}t_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we apply <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> again, since <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, we first apply <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we consider the word <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(t_{3})f_{0}(t_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(t_{3})f_{0}(t_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding image is <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(t_{3})f_{0}(t_{4})=t_{5}\cdot t_{6}t_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(t_{3})f_{0}(t_{4})=t_{5}\cdot t_{6}t_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}(2)=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}(2)=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Again, since <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, applying <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> leads to <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}(t_{5})f_{0}(t_{6})f_{1}(t_{7})=t_{8}\cdot t_{9}t_{10}\cdot t_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}(t_{5})f_{0}(t_{6})f_{1}(t_{7})=t_{8}\cdot t_{9}t_{10}\cdot t_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{3}(2)=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{3}(2)=8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the next iteration, since <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{3}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{3}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is even, the image is <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(t_{8})f_{1}(t_{9})f_{0}(t_{10})f_{1}(t_{11})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(t_{8})f_{1}(t_{9})f_{0}(t_{10})f_{1}(t_{11})</annotation></semantics></math> and so on and so forth.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that in the tree in <a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> can be recovered as follows: we consider the leftmost path in the (infinite) subtree rooted at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this subtree, the leftmost vertex at level <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the particular substitution (<a href="#S1.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Definition 3 (Our sequence of interest). ‣ 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) but only on the structure of the numeration tree.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since we apply <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> alternatively, we observe that if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n_{k+1}(j)}t_{n_{k+1}(j)+1}=f_{0}(t_{n_{k}(j)})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n_{k+1}(j)}t_{n_{k+1}(j)+1}=f_{0}(t_{n_{k}(j)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and if otherwise <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n_{k+1}(j)}=f_{1}(t_{n_{k}(j)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n_{k+1}(j)}=f_{1}(t_{n_{k}(j)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Note that arbitrarily long prefixes of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by iterating <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> starting from the symbol <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟶</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since these prefixes start at position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the first morphism applied at each iteration is always <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> also occurs at an odd position of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then every factor of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at both even and odd positions in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see this, observe that if a factor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> odd in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> at positions <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j+i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, these two occurrences have opposite parities.
This reduces the problem to proving that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at an odd position of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, by the discussion above, we must find <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}(j),\dots,n_{\ell}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}(j),\dots,n_{\ell}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> are even, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell+1}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell+1}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j}={\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j}={\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, it is the parity that determines which morphism is applied first, so this <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> would produce <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}(t_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}(t_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> at position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell+1}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell+1}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, whence the result since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j}={\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j}={\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary integer.
First, we note that if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(2^{\ell}j)=3^{k}\cdot 2^{\ell-k}\cdot j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(2^{\ell}j)=3^{k}\cdot 2^{\ell-k}\cdot j</annotation></semantics></math> is even for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell}(2^{\ell}j)=3^{\ell}\cdot j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell}(2^{\ell}j)=3^{\ell}\cdot j</annotation></semantics></math> has the same parity as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, it suffices to find <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2^{\ell}j}={\rm 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2^{\ell}j}={\rm 0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible modulo <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell+2}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell+1}i=2^{\ell+1}-1\pmod{2^{\ell+2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell+1}i=2^{\ell+1}-1\pmod{2^{\ell+2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We note that this congruence ensures, in particular, that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{0}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{0}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the integer satisfying <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell+1}i=2^{\ell+1}-1+2^{\ell+2}j_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell+1}i=2^{\ell+1}-1+2^{\ell+2}j_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=2j_{0}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=2j_{0}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is odd, and note that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell+1}i+1=2^{\ell+1}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell+1}i+1=2^{\ell+1}j</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now show that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{2^{\ell}j}\neq t_{2^{\ell}i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{2^{\ell}j}\neq t_{2^{\ell}i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Once this is established, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an occurrence of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟶</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math> at either position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell}i</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell}j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since both <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are odd, it follows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at an odd position of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, completing the argument.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=t_{2^{\ell}i}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=t_{2^{\ell}i}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell}i</annotation></semantics></math> is even and since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(c)=cc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(c)=cc</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,2^{\ell}i])=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,2^{\ell}i)\,c)=\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i)f_{0}(c)=\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i)\,cc." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,2^{\ell}i])=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,2^{\ell}i)\,c)=\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i)f_{0}(c)=\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i)\,cc.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">So <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> appears in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 2^{\ell-1}i</annotation></semantics></math> is even and we can iterate our argument: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> also occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{2}\cdot 2^{\ell-2}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{2}\cdot 2^{\ell-2}i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Continuing like this, we get that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell}i</annotation></semantics></math>, which is odd since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
Therefore, if we apply <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> one more time, since the parity changes, we then get</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i])=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i)\,c)=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))f_{1}(c)=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))\,\bar{c}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i])=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i)\,c)=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))f_{1}(c)=\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))\,\bar{c},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">so the bit-wise complement of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> also occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now focus on the position of this occurrence.
The length of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed using the fact that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell}i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell}i-1</annotation></semantics></math> is even since in that case we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))|=|\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i-1))|+|f_{0}(t_{3^{\ell}i-1})|=\frac{3}{2}(3^{\ell}i-1)+2=2^{\ell}j," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i))|=|\Phi(\mathbf{t}_{3/2}[0,3^{\ell}i-1))|+|f_{0}(t_{3^{\ell}i-1})|=\frac{3}{2}(3^{\ell}i-1)+2=2^{\ell}j,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">where in the last step we use that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3^{\ell+1}i+1=2^{\ell+1}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3^{\ell+1}i+1=2^{\ell+1}j</annotation></semantics></math> by definition of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
We conclude that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{c}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell}j</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> in position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\ell}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\ell}i</annotation></semantics></math>, which is enough.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We present an alternative proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary 13. ‣ Uniform recurrence. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.
It does not rely on the difference word and appears to be more general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Second proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary 13. ‣ Uniform recurrence. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin with some definitions.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed point of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S1.E5" title="In Definition 3 (Our sequence of interest). ‣ 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> starting from the letter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟷</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{3/2}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\tau^{n}({\tt 1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{3/2}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\tau^{n}({\tt 1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">For an infinite word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> the set of its finite factors.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">A classical result in topological dynamics (every system contains a minimal subsystem; see Auslander’s book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>) ensures the existence of a uniformly recurrent infinite word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})\subseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})\subseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the quantities <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, from the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 14. ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, and define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}(j):=(n_{0}(j)\bmod 2,n_{1}(j)\bmod 2,\dots,n_{k}(j)\bmod 2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}(j):=(n_{0}(j)\bmod 2,n_{1}(j)\bmod 2,\dots,n_{k}(j)\bmod 2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof consists of three steps.
First, we show that for each <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is periodic with least period <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, we use this to prove that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> contains either <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{s}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{s}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we deduce the uniform recurrence of both <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1.<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We begin<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                      <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                      
                      
                      
                      
                      
                      
                      
                    We present the argument for the sake of completeness, it appears in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Lem. 4.14]</cite>.</span></span></span> with the periodicity of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}j)=3^{\ell}\cdot 2^{k+1-\ell}\cdot j\equiv 0\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}j)=3^{\ell}\cdot 2^{k+1-\ell}\cdot j\equiv 0\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}(2^{k+1}j)=N_{k}(2^{k+1}j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}(2^{k+1}j)=N_{k}(2^{k+1}j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,j^{\prime}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,j^{\prime}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary and write <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2^{k+1}j+j_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2^{k+1}j+j_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq j_{0}&lt;2^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq j_{0}&lt;2^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p+2^{k+1}p^{\prime})=n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p)+n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p+2^{k+1}p^{\prime})=n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p)+n_{\ell}(2^{k+1}p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\ell}(i)=n_{\ell}(i^{\prime})\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\ell}(i)=n_{\ell}(i^{\prime})\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
whenever <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv i^{\prime}\pmod{2^{k+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv i^{\prime}\pmod{2^{k+1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This proves that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is periodic with period <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be the least period of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}(j))_{j\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, note that for any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k-1}(2^{k}j)=3^{k}\cdot j\equiv j\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k-1}(2^{k}j)=3^{k}\cdot j\equiv j\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}(2^{k}j))_{j\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}(2^{k}j))_{j\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a constant sequence.
Therefore, if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq 2^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq 2^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}(2^{k}j))_{j\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}(2^{k}j))_{j\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, which is a contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2.<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Consider the maps <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S1.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Definition 3 (Our sequence of interest). ‣ 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
For <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define maps <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi_{i}(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1})=f_{i\bmod{2}}(a_{0})f_{{i+1}\bmod{2}}(a_{1})\cdots f_{{i+k-1}\bmod{2}}(a_{k-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1})=f_{i\bmod{2}}(a_{0})f_{{i+1}\bmod{2}}(a_{1})\cdots f_{{i+k-1}\bmod{2}}(a_{k-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all non-empty words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we show that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{s}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{s}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix a positive integer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
By definition of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can construct a <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold desubstitution of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>: there exist
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\dots,i_{k}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\dots,i_{k}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi_{i_{k}}\circ\cdots\circ\Phi_{i_{1}}(\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i_{k}}\circ\cdots\circ\Phi_{i_{1}}(\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> after deleting a finite prefix.
Now, by the periodicity from the first step, there exists a position <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math> such that reapplying the substitutions
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i_{k}},\dots,\Phi_{i_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i_{k}},\dots,\Phi_{i_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0}^{k}(\mathbf{y}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0}^{k}(\mathbf{y}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Equivalently, this is possible because all subtrees occur in the numeration system.)
Repeating this procedure for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain occurrences in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> of words of the form <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0}^{k}(a_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0}^{k}(a_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for some letters <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the pigeonhole principle, there exists a letter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> that appears infinitely often in the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{k})_{k\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{k})_{k\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0}^{k}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0}^{k}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> are arbitrarily long prefixes of the fixed point of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> starting with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> contains the language of that fixed point, which we know is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p15.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>
This completes the second step.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3.<span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Finally, we prove that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly recurrent.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the bit-wise complement of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, one is uniformly recurrent if and only if the other is.
It therefore suffices to show that the fixed point starting with the letter <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in the previous paragraph is uniformly recurrent.
By symmetry, we may assume <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a={\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a={\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})\supseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊇</mo><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})\supseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be a factor of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly recurrent, there exists <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that every factor of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> of length at least <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> contains an occurrence of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cL(\mathbf{x})\supseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊇</mo><mrow><mi>Fac</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cL(\mathbf{x})\supseteq\cL(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the same holds for factors of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> of length at least <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly recurrent.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bit-wise complement.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The second main result of this section is to show that the sets of factors of both words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are closed under bit-wise complement, i.e., if a word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a factor of one of the words, so is <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of factors of <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus of <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) is closed under bit-wise complement.
Consequently, the relationship between the factor complexities of <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}(n+1)=2\mathsf{p}_{\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}(n+1)=2\mathsf{p}_{\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We continue using the maps <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> from the beginning of the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 14. ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.
A direct computation shows that
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f_{i}(a)}=f_{i}(\bar{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f_{i}(a)}=f_{i}(\bar{a})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows by induction that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\Phi(u)}=\Phi(\bar{u})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\Phi(u)}=\Phi(\bar{u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all nonempty words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that, for any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the bitwise complement of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, are arbitrarily long prefixes of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed under bitwise complement if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">To show this, we proceed by induction on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the base case <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{0}({\tt 1})={\rm 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{0}({\tt 1})={\rm 1}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the induction step, assume that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, by <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 14. ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in an even position of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\ell+1}({\tt 1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\ell+1}({\tt 1})</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the previous result and <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7 ([]). ‣ 2.1 On the sequence of first differences ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we obtain the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The factor complexities <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}_{\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝗉</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}_{\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathbf{t}_{3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> are both in <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{\log 3}{\log(3/2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{\log 3}{\log(3/2)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reversal.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The third main result of this section is to show that the sets of factors of both words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are closed under reversal.
The <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reversal</span> <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of a word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon^{R}=\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon^{R}=\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{\ell-1})^{R}=a_{\ell-1}a_{\ell-2}\cdots a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{\ell-1})^{R}=a_{\ell-1}a_{\ell-2}\cdots a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are symbols.
Equivalently, the map <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto u^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto u^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique map <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\colon\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}\to\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\colon\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}\to\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(uv)^{R}=v^{R}u^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(uv)^{R}=v^{R}u^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We propose two proofs of <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Proposition 17. ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>: a standalone one using the same arguments as in the proof of and another one relying on the properties of the tree associated with the numeration.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 17</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of factors of <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are closed under reversal, i.e., if a word <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a factor of one of the words, so is <math id="Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">First proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Proposition 17. ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the maps <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S1.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Definition 3 (Our sequence of interest). ‣ 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
For <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define maps <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Second proof of . ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi_{i}(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1})=f_{i\bmod{2}}(a_{0})f_{{i+1}\bmod{2}}(a_{1})\cdots f_{{i+k-1}\bmod{2}}(a_{k-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}(a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1})=f_{i\bmod{2}}(a_{0})f_{{i+1}\bmod{2}}(a_{1})\cdots f_{{i+k-1}\bmod{2}}(a_{k-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all non-empty words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}a_{1}\cdots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A direct inspection shows that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{i}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{i}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> is a palindrome for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{i}(a)\big)^{R}=\Phi_{i}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{i}(a)\big)^{R}=\Phi_{i}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, for any <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and any nonempty word <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=a_{0}a_{1}\dots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=a_{0}a_{1}\dots a_{k-1}\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\big(\Phi_{i}(u)\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big(\Phi_{i}(u)\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex56.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\big(\Phi_{i}(a_{0})\Phi_{i+1}(a_{1})\cdots\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\big(\Phi_{i}(a_{0})\Phi_{i+1}(a_{1})\cdots\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\big(\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\big)^{R}\,\big(\Phi_{i+k-2}(a_{k-2})\big)^{R}\cdots\big(\Phi_{i}(a_{0})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\big(\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\big)^{R}\,\big(\Phi_{i+k-2}(a_{k-2})\big)^{R}\cdots\big(\Phi_{i}(a_{0})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\,\Phi_{i+k-2}(a_{k-2})\cdots\Phi_{i}(a_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Phi_{i+k-1}(a_{k-1})\,\Phi_{i+k-2}(a_{k-2})\cdots\Phi_{i}(a_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Phi_{k-1-i}(u^{R})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Phi_{k-1-i}(u^{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">We use this identity to prove closure under reversal.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the words <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, are arbitrarily long prefixes of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to show that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We again proceed by induction on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the base case <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this is clear because <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{0}({\tt 0})={\tt 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟶</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{0}({\tt 0})={\tt 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a palindrome.
For the induction step, assume that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the identity above, we have
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell+1}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}=\Phi_{k-1}(\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell+1}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}=\Phi_{k-1}(\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=|\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=|\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, by the induction hypothesis, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus, by <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Proposition 14. ‣ Occurences at odd and even positions. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, it occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> at parity <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{k-1}(\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{k-1}(\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell}({\tt 0})\big)^{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell+1}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟶</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\Phi_{0}^{\ell+1}({\tt 0})\big)^{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, occurs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For readers familiar with rational base numeration systems and, in particular, with the properties of the numeration tree described in <a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1.3 The base 3/2 and the corresponding Thue–Morse word ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, we propose an alternative proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Proposition 17. ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Second proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Proposition 17. ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">All finite admissible subtrees (i.e., those determined by the periodic rhythm <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of the vertices degrees in the breadth-first traversal) appear in the tree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. In particular, for each <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, one finds a tree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of height <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> whose leftmost vertex at each level has degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. One also finds a “symmetric” tree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> whose rightmost vertex at each level has degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. This information completely determines the structure of the two subtrees.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, at each level of the subtree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>), starting from the root, one begins with a vertex of degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> that is the leftmost (resp., the rightmost) on that level. Then, vertices of degree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> alternate starting from the left (resp., right). One can show by induction that at each level, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same number of vertices. Since the construction of each level proceeds from left to right in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and from right to left in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, then, at each level, the sequence of vertex degrees in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reversal of that in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In <a href="#S2.F6" title="In Second proof of . ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we have depicted two such subtrees of height <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> with root <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,c\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>𝟶</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟷</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,c\in\{{\tt 0},{\tt 1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (we only record the parity of the vertices).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first subtree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in the proof of <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Proposition 15. ‣ Bit-wise complement. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, produces a prefix of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{3/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{t}_{3/2}}</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on the parity of the root), which is read on the last level. In <a href="#S2.F6" title="In Second proof of . ‣ Reversal. ‣ 2.2 From the sequence Δ(𝐭_{3/2}) back to the original sequence 𝐭_{3/2} ‣ 2 Combinatorial properties of 𝐭_{3/2} and 𝐭^′ ‣ Symbols frequencies in the Thue–Morse word in base 3/2 and related conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we see the factor <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aa\bar{a}\bar{a}\bar{a}a\bar{a}\bar{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aa\bar{a}\bar{a}\bar{a}a\bar{a}\bar{a}</annotation></semantics></math> on the last level of the tree depicted on the left. The symmetric subtree <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> then produces the reversal word. One can then conclude since the set of factors is closed under bit-wise complement, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
